WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.298 --> 00:00:02.090
SPEAKER: Service Level
Objectives, or SLOs,

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.650
are the primary way by which
we set appropriate reliability

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.330
targets for our services.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.610
The latest release of SLO
monitoring in Google Cloud

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.370
operations makes doing this
especially easy for services

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.240
running on GKE.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.490
I'm here today to give you
a walk through and show you

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.490
how to get started.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.656
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:17.656 --> 00:00:19.548
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.798 --> 00:00:26.090
We looked at how to use the
API to define Service Level

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.600
Indicators, or SLIs and set
SLOs in an earlier episode.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.880
But now, you can do
both of those in the UI.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.570
Let's take a look.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.030
Start by going to the Services
screen under Monitoring.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.510
If you were using the Istio
service mesh in your cluster,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.177
you'll notice that your services
have already been automatically

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:42.380
identified for you.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.190
You can filter the
list as necessary.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.130
For example, you
may want to only see

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.110
services running in
a particular cluster

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.730
or in that particular region.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.400
To filter for services that
match a particular label value,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.178
just type the value of the label
you want into the Filter field.

00:00:55.178 --> 00:00:57.470
If you're not using Istio,
you can define a new service

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.690
by selecting a GKE
entity you'd like to use

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.722
as the base for the service.

00:01:01.722 --> 00:01:03.680
This won't make any
modifications of the entity

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.030
itself, it'll just create
a new monitoring construct

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.608
you can use to create SLOs.

00:01:07.608 --> 00:01:09.650
Once you've found the
service you're looking for,

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.230
or created a new one,
click on it in the list.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.660
On the Service
Detail screen, you

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.993
can see the SLOs you've already
defined for it, if there

00:01:15.993 --> 00:01:18.680
are any, any alerts for
the time span selected,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.300
and the logs for the service.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.890
From here click Create SLO.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.990
In order to create
an SLO, you first

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:28.360
have to choose the SLI, or
the Service Level Indicator,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.940
you'd like to use as the
measure of service reliability.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.670
Here you have three options:
availability, performance,

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:34.570
or other.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.370
But what does each
one of those mean?

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.660
An Availability SLI
will measure the ratio

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.720
of successful requests to
all request of the service.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.633
For this service,
Istio automatically

00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:46.800
tracks that metric and no
additional instrumentation

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.910
is necessary.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.470
A Performance SLI
will measure the ratio

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.470
of requests that complete
within a specific time

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.910
to all requests.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.860
Because Istio automatically
tracks latency

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.290
for each request, this is
another out of the box metric.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.640
If you want to use a
custom metric as your SLI,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.187
select Other at this point.

00:02:03.187 --> 00:02:05.270
For example, you may have
instrumented the service

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.730
using OpenTelemetry to count
the number of transactions

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.710
that meet specific criteria.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.690
Using a custom SLI will
let you select that metric.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.680
The next choice is
between a Request-based

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.357
and a Window-based SLI.

00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:18.940
We covered this in
our earlier episode

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.230
on using the API to create
SLOs, but it's worth

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.940
reviewing it briefly.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.980
Request-based SLIs
measure the fraction

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.950
of good units of work
against the total.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.450
Now for example, what percentage
of requests of your service

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.670
were successful in
a given time window?

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.620
An example of a
Request-based availability

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.520
SLO is 99% of requests are
successful over a rolling seven

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.155
day window.

00:02:38.155 --> 00:02:39.530
This is the more
straightforward,

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.570
and commonly used, option.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.600
Window-based SLIs measure the
fraction of time intervals

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.180
during which the service meets
some threshold over a given

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:48.500
time window.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.420
An example of a
Window-based availability

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.200
SLO is 99% of requests
are successful within 99%

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.790
of 10 minute intervals over
a rolling seven day window.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.470
Once you've selected your
SLI and evaluation type,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:01.365
click Next.

00:03:01.365 --> 00:03:02.990
Take a look at the
signal you've chosen

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.990
and confirm that the metric
behaves as you would expect.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.660
For example, if the service has
been healthy for the last hour

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.940
the metric should show
a mostly flat trend.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.990
Once you're satisfied with
the signal, click Next.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.240
You may recall that an SLO
is the combination of three

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.880
things: the SLI, which
we've already chosen,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.853
a target, and a
compliance period.

00:03:20.853 --> 00:03:23.270
On this screen, you need to
provide the compliance period,

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.853
which is the length
of the time window

00:03:24.853 --> 00:03:27.110
over which the SLI is
going to be evaluated.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.910
Next, specify your goal.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:30.700
This is the target for the SLI.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.870
Now your SLO
definition is complete.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.880
Click Next to finalize it.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.400
This is the last
step: give your SLO

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.327
a name that reflects what it's
measuring, and click Submit.

00:03:39.327 --> 00:03:41.410
That'll take you back to
the Service Detail screen

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:46.120
with your new SLO added and
its health clearly displayed.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.290
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.660
We looked through the new
SLO monitoring functionality

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.760
and learned how to use
it to define availability

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.917
and performance SLOs for
our services running in GKE.

00:03:54.917 --> 00:03:56.500
Now you are ready
to start configuring

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.450
SLOs for your own services.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.588
Thanks for watching and
come back soon for more.

00:04:00.588 --> 00:04:01.630
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.870
Stay healthy out there.

